Q: @google/genai SDK: Does chat.sendMessage config override the ai.chats.create config? A: <p>Modify your script to store the base configuration and spread it into the <code>sendMessage</code> call:</p>
<p>JavaScript</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script setup&gt;
import { ref } from 'vue';
import { GoogleGenAI } from &quot;@google/genai&quot;;

const API_KEY = &quot;YOUR_API_KEY&quot;;
const genAI = new GoogleGenAI(API_KEY);

// Define your base config once
const BASE_CONFIG = {
  systemInstruction: &quot;You are an expert about tennis. Don't reply to other kinds of requests...&quot;,
};

const chat = genAI.getGenerativeModel({ 
  model: &quot;gemini-1.5-flash&quot;, 
  ...BASE_CONFIG 
}).startChat();

const inputText = ref(&quot;&quot;);
const result = ref(&quot;&quot;);
let abortController = null;

async function sendRequest() {
  // Always instantiate a fresh controller for a new request
  abortController = new AbortController();

  try {
    const result = await chat.sendMessage(inputText.value, {
      // SPREAD the base config here to ensure systemInstructions persist
      ...BASE_CONFIG,
      signal: abortController.signal 
    });

    const response = await result.response;
    result.value = response.text();
  } 
  catch (e) {
    if (e.name === &quot;AbortError&quot;) {
      console.log(&quot;Request aborted&quot;);
    } else {
      console.error(e);
    }
  } finally {
    abortController = null;
  }
}
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>A few notes on your implementation:</h3>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>SDK naming:</strong> Ensure you are using <code>getGenerativeModel</code> and <code>startChat()</code>. The method <code>ai.chats.create</code> looks like it might be from an older beta or a slightly different wrapper; the official Google JS SDK standard is <code>model.startChat()</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Signal vs AbortSignal:</strong> In the most recent versions of the SDK, the property in the request options is often just named <code>signal</code> (to match the fetch API standard), though <code>abortSignal</code> is sometimes supported for backward compatibility.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Ref-counting:</strong> Since you are in Vue, if the component unmounts while a request is active, make sure to call <code>abortRequest()</code> in an <code>onUnmounted</code> hook to prevent memory leaks or state updates on destroyed components.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>It’s definitely not the most &quot;ergonomic&quot; design on the SDK's part, but spreading the config is the standard architectural pattern to ensure the model maintains its &quot;personality&quot; across interrupted requests.</p>
